 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Early Training Instability:** NeRF renderings are inaccurate early in training. To mitigate this, 25% of the time, a training pose is used, and the RGB component is sampled directly from the training image [S1].
-*   **Over-smoothing:** The DDM regularizer can cause over-smoothing of thin structures and hallucinate details in scene regions not visible in any training view, which significantly impacts reconstruction scores [S1].
-*   **Scheduling Sensitivity:** The diffusion time parameter $\tau$ and DDM gradient weights require careful scheduling to produce good results; improper scheduling leads to poor convergence [S1].
-*   **Shiny Objects:** The density-based approach struggles with shiny objects while excelling on diffuse/textured surfaces [S1].
+*   **Early Training Inaccuracy:** NeRF renderings are inaccurate early in training. To mitigate this, 25% of the time, a training pose is used, and the RGB component is sampled directly from the training image [S1].
+*   **Over-smoothing:** The DDM regularizer can cause over-smoothing of thin structures [S1].
+*   **Hallucination:** The method may hallucinate details in scene regions not visible in any training view, which can negatively impact reconstruction scores [S1].
+*   **Patch Size Sensitivity:** A $24 \times 24$ patch size outperforms $48 \times 48$ on the LLFF dataset but underperforms on DTU [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** NeRF optimization takes approximately 12,000 steps [S1]. On a single Nvidia A100 GPU, training takes approximately 30 minutes per scene [S1]. The use of Instant NGP allows for fast rendering of large patches ($48 \times 48$), which is critical for the efficiency of this method compared to methods using smaller patches [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Uses multi-scale hash tables (Instant NGP) which store feature encodings in a fixed memory block, reducing the MLP size compared to standard coordinate-based MLPs [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Improves reconstruction quality on LLFF and DTU datasets compared to baselines without DDM regularization [S1]. It mitigates artifacts like "floaters" and improves generalization to novel views [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Directly regularizes geometry via RGBD patches; leverages strong priors from synthetic data (Hypersim); fast rendering allows larger context patches [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Can over-smooth thin structures; hallucinates details in occluded regions; sensitive to hyperparameter scheduling; struggles with shiny objects [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** Limited-view scenarios where standard NeRFs produce poor geometry or artifacts; scenes with diffuse/textured surfaces [S1, S2].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Scenes with significant specular/shiny surfaces [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The method adds the cost of rendering RGBD patches and passing them through the DDM to each NeRF optimization step. Instant NGP's fast rendering mitigates the patch rendering cost [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the DDM parameters and the Instant NGP hash tables [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Improves geometry quality and generalization to novel views on LLFF and DTU datasets compared to baselines without DDM regularization [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Mitigates "floaters" and low-quality geometry in limited-view scenarios; leverages strong priors from synthetic data [S1, S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Can over-smooth thin structures; may hallucinate details in occluded regions; requires careful scheduling of $\tau$ and $\lambda_{\text{DDM}}$ [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** When training NeRFs with limited input views and seeking improved geometric plausibility [S1, S2].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When high-fidelity reconstruction of thin structures is critical and over-smoothing is unacceptable; when computational resources for DDM inference are limited [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Diffusion-regularized NeRF (DiffusioNeRF) was introduced to address the under-constrained nature of NeRF geometry and color fields, particularly with few input views [S1]. The method leverages Denoising Diffusion Models (DDMs), which were originally developed for image generation, as score function estimators for RGBD patch distributions [S1]. It was trained on the Hypersim synthetic dataset [S1]. The approach was implemented using torch-ngp (Instant NGP) with the tiny-cuda-tc backend [S1]. It is used for novel-view synthesis and 3D reconstruction [S1]. A related generalizable framework using DDM regularization was also evaluated on the DTU dataset [S2].
+*   **Origin:** Proposed in "DiffusioNeRF: Regularizing Neural Radiance Fields with Denoising Diffusion Models" (CVPR 2023) [S1].
+*   **Development:** Builds on the understanding that NeRFs are under-constrained with few views, leading to artifacts [S1]. It leverages the connection between DDM noise estimation and score matching [S1].
+*   **Usage:** Implemented using torch-ngp (Instant NGP) with tiny-cuda-tiny backend [S1]. Evaluated on LLFF and DTU datasets [S1]. Also referenced in generalizable NeRF frameworks trained on DTU [S2].
 
